{"id": "q0225", "pool": "frozen", "arm": "aggressive", "tier": "aggressive", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 3936810795, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "c1188d4ab398808355719c420f025bd920b27aba798f5596eb328457a751cc63", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": 0.5533501505851746, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 0, "query": "Assume that all gases are perfect and that data refer to 298.15 K unless otherwise stated. Calculate the change in chemical potential of a perfect gas when its pressure is increased isothermally from $1.8 \\mathrm{~atm}$ to $29.5 \\mathrm{~atm}$ at $40^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.\n\nA. +10.2$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nB. +7.3$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nC. +8.4$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nD. +1.9$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nE. +5.9$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nF. +2.6$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nG. +6.2$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nH. +4.8$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nI. +3.5$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\nJ. +9.1$\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$\n\nAnswer with the correct option letter and its content.", "input_path": "/data/audio_pool/q0225.wav", "input_sha256": "58f3ac33eb0a0b30608a603f8b6bfbb0dbedd74d1fb76b26d000d1f3bfe00024", "input_samples": 779200, "input_s": 48.7, "n_real_chunks": 49, "score": 0.8741, "act": 0.9993, "is_info": true, "fired": true, "mode": "escalated", "answer": "", "relay_text": "Using the stated pressures: \\[ \\Delta \\mu = RT\\ln\\left(\\frac{p2}{p1}\\right) \\] \\[ T=44.0^\\circ C=317.15\\text{ K} \\] \\[ p1=1.8\\text{ atm},\\quad p2=29.5\\text{ mbar}=\\frac{29.5}{1013.25}\\text{ atm}=0.0291\\text{ atm} \\] \\[ \\Delta \\mu=(8.314)(317.15)\\ln\\left(\\frac{0.0291}{1.8}\\right) \\] \\[ \\Delta \\mu \\approx -1.09\\times 10^4\\text{ J mol}^{-1} = -10.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1} \\] So the chemical potential decreases by about 10.9 kJ mol⁻¹. If the intended final pressure was 29.5 atm rather than 29.5 mbar, the answer would be about +7.4 kJ mol⁻¹, corresponding to option B if its unit should be kJ, not J.", "expert_answer": "Using the stated pressures:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta \\mu = RT\\ln\\left(\\frac{p_2}{p_1}\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\nT=44.0^\\circ C=317.15\\text{ K}\n\\]\n\n\\[\np_1=1.8\\text{ atm},\\quad p_2=29.5\\text{ mbar}=\\frac{29.5}{1013.25}\\text{ atm}=0.0291\\text{ atm}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta \\mu=(8.314)(317.15)\\ln\\left(\\frac{0.0291}{1.8}\\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\Delta \\mu \\approx -1.09\\times 10^4\\text{ J mol}^{-1}\n= -10.9\\text{ kJ mol}^{-1}\n\\]\n\nSo the chemical potential **decreases by about 10.9 kJ mol⁻¹**.\n\n**None of the listed options matches the stated data.** If the intended final pressure was **29.5 atm** rather than **29.5 mbar**, the answer would be about **+7.4 kJ mol⁻¹**, corresponding to option **B if its unit should be kJ, not J**.", "transcript": "Assume that all gases are perfect and that data refer to 298.15 K unless otherwise stated. Calculate the change in chemical potential of a perfect gas when its pressure is increased isothermally from 1.8 atm to 29.5 mbar at 44.0 °C. A. plus 10.2 kJ mol−1, B. plus 7.3 J mol−1, C. 8.4 kJ mol−1, D. plu", "asr_s": 1.25, "expert_s": 9.49, "expert_input_samples": 464000, "expert_input_s": 29.0, "expert_input_sha256": "dfd9b41ef19b7e95e6d70db054fe3145f3f7879e044e0f831d42ec6feb010707", "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": 8.442, "relay_synth_ms": 154675, "onset_chunk": 28, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 2, "feed_lag_max_ms": 103, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 164, "audio_files": {"relay": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0225_aggressive_relay.wav", "sha256": "ab1d72db9db3b92b04edf4f4a7763e30a5a9cf8b6221e137d29e73b64d2bc21a", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 292.28}, "stall": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/frozen/audio/q0225_aggressive_stall.wav", "sha256": "8330d189201c5181231260b9cbeb2f34d704d9fe200b19bcd9a9f8dc5ce249f6", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 2.96}}, "chunks": [[0, 1